<SubSection title="Scale Models" id="Scale Models">
    <p>In many engineering applications, directly testing a full-scale system may be
too expensive, impractical, or difficult to control. Instead, smaller
laboratory models are often constructed and tested under controlled conditions,
such as aircraft testing in wind tunnels.</p>
    <p>The purpose of a scale model is not simply to create a smaller geometric copy
of the real system, but rather to reproduce the important flow physics of the
prototype. This is accomplished by matching the appropriate dimensionless
groups between the model and the real system or in other words, equating pi terms.
Given that a physical relationship is written in dimensionless form as:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="\\Pi_1=\\phi(\\Pi_2,\\Pi_3,\\ldots)" title="" />

If the relevant dimensionless groups are the same for both the model and the
prototype,

<DisplayEquation equation="(\\Pi_2)_m=(\\Pi_2)_p, \\qquad (\\Pi_3)_m=(\\Pi_3)_p, \\qquad \\ldots" title="" />

then the remaining dimensionless term (which is usually constructed as the dependent variable) should also match:

<DisplayEquation equation="(\\Pi_1)_m=(\\Pi_1)_p" title="" />

In practice this means that if  one develops conditions for a scale model characterized by specific values for dimensionless variables, then one can use these results to predict the outcome for a similar full-scale system whose flow physics is defined by the same dimensionless parameters. For example, consider flow over a body where the drag relationship depends on
the Reynolds number as before:

<DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{F_D}{\\rho V^2L^2}=\\phi(Re)" title="" />

To preserve similar flow behavior between the model and prototype, the Reynolds
numbers should match:

<DisplayEquation equation="(Re)_m=(Re)_p" title="" />

or

<DisplayEquation equation="\\left( \\frac{\\rho VL}{\\mu} \\right)_m = \\left( \\frac{\\rho VL}{\\mu} \\right)_p" title="" />

If the same fluid is used in both cases, then density and viscosity are the
same, giving:

<DisplayEquation equation="V_mL_m=V_pL_p" title="" />

This means that a smaller model may require a larger velocity in order to
maintain the same Reynolds number and preserve similar fluid behavior.

    <Image src='/fluid/dimensional_analysis/geometricanddynamicsimilarity.jpeg' width='7'>Conditions for model similitude</Image>

Most of the time, in developing pi terms and dimensionless quantities, one will notice that they fall into one of two categories. Oftentimes one of the pi terms will express some geometric result, such as the ratio of two length such as height and width for instance

<DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{h}{w}" title="" />

This shows that one part of establishing similitude between a laboratory and full-scale model is about establishing geometric similarity - equating the shape or profile a test subject. In the above example, it was also seen that a matching Reynolds number was required, indicating that we needed to equate flow conditions or physics as governed by flow variables and fluid properties. Thus a second category of similarity is the dynamic similarity. This usually bears out as matching the dimensionless values of the Reynolds number,
Mach number, or Froude number. These two categories make sense; the laboratory model must recreate the same geometric features and flow physics as the corresponding full-scale prototype in order to make accurate predictions.
    </SubSection>
<SubSection title="Non-Dimensionalizing Equations" id="Non-Dimensionalizing Equations">
    <p>Another important application of dimensional analysis is rewriting governing
equations and exact solutions in dimensionless form. This process is called
non-dimensionalization.</p>
    <p>The basic idea is to scale variables using characteristic reference quantities.
For example, a velocity may be scaled by a characteristic speed <InlineEquation equation="V" />, a length
by a characteristic length <InlineEquation equation="L" />, a time by a characteristic flow time <InlineEquation equation="L/V" />,
and a pressure by a characteristic pressure scale <InlineEquation equation="\\rho V^2" />:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="u^*=\\frac{u}{V}, \\qquad x^*=\\frac{x}{L}, \\qquad t^*=\\frac{t}{L/V}, \\qquad p^*=\\frac{p}{\\rho V^2}" title="" />

    <p>The starred quantities are dimensionless variables that have been normalized with respect to their reference quantities.</p>
    <p>Once the dimensionless variables are defined, they are substituted into the
original governing equations. The equations are then algebraically rearranged
so that all remaining terms are dimensionless. During this process,
dimensionless groups naturally emerge from the mathematics itself.
For example, the incompressible Navier--Stokes equations may be written as:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="\\rho \\left( \\frac{\\partial \\vec{V}}{\\partial t} + (\\vec{V}\\cdot\\nabla)\\vec{V} \\right) = -\\nabla p + \\mu\\nabla^2\\vec{V}" title="" />

After introducing characteristic scales and rewriting the equation in
dimensionless form, the result becomes:

<DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{\\partial \\vec{V}^*}{\\partial t^*} + (\\vec{V}^*\\cdot\\nabla^*)\\vec{V}^* = -\\nabla^* p^* + \\frac{1}{Re}\\nabla^{*2}\\vec{V}^*" title="" />

where

<DisplayEquation equation="Re=\\frac{\\rho VL}{\\mu}" title="" />

    <p>is the now familiar Reynolds number. The reciprocal of the Reynolds number now appears directly as the coefficient multiplying the
viscous term. Large Reynolds numbers therefore correspond to flows where
inertial effects dominate, while small Reynolds numbers indicate flows where
viscous effects are more important, just as was discussed earlier.</p>
    <p>Dimensionless forms are also useful when rewriting exact solutions. For
example, capillary rise (as was touched on in the introductory reference content) in a tube may be written as:</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="h=\\frac{2\\sigma\\cos\\theta}{\\rho g R}" title="" />

Dividing by a characteristic height scale which is the height for perfect wetting or a contact angle of zero,

<DisplayEquation equation="h_0=\\frac{2\\sigma}{\\rho g R}" title="" />

gives the dimensionless form:

<DisplayEquation equation="h^*=\\frac{h}{h_0}=\\cos\\theta" title="" />

where

<DisplayEquation equation="h^*=\\frac{h}{h_0}" title="" />

    <p>is the dimensionless height.</p>
    <p>Expressing equations and solutions in dimensionless form generally doesn't chart new progress in solving an analytic equation. Nevertheless, it often simplifies the
mathematics, highlights the dominant physics, and allows the results to apply
to a broader class of similar flow problems.</p>
</SubSection>
